It is time to get some prespective on the concept of the derivative. 
In the case of a (differentiable) scalar-valued function of a single cariable, f:X ⊆ R -> R, the derivative f'(a) is simply a real number, the slope of the tangent line to the graph of f at the point (a, f(a)). 
From a more sophisticated (and slightly less geometric) point of view, the derivative f'(a) is the number such that the function

		h(x) = f(a) + f'(a) (x - a)

is a good linear approximation to f(x) for x near a. 
(And, of course, y = h(x) is the equation of the tangent line.)
If a function f:X ⊆ R^n -> R of n variables is differentiable, there must exist n parital derivatives (δf)/(δx₁), ..., (δf)/(δsub(x sub(n))). 
These partial derivatives from the components of the gradient vector ∇f (or the entries of the 1 x n matrix Df). 
It is the gradient that should properly be considered to be the derivative of f, but the following sense: ∇f(a) is the vector such that the function h:R^n -> R given by 

	h(x) = f(a) + ∇f(a)*(x - a)

is a good linear approximation to f(x) for x near a. 
Finally, the derivative of a differentiable vector-valued function f:X ⊆ R^n -> R^m may be taken to be the matrix Df of partial derivatives, but in the sense that the function h:R^n -> R^m given by 

		h(x) = f(a) + Df(a) (x - a)

is a good linear approximation to f(x) near a. 
You should view the derivative Df(a) not as a "static" matrix of numbers, but rather as a matrix that defines a linear mapping from R^n to R^n. 
(See example 5 of 1.6)
